  "technical_issues": [
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "A sun-synchronous orbit provides approximately constant local solar time at a given latitude; it does not guarantee continuous sunlight. The report's claims of a constant sunlight supply and that the descending path is constantly in sunlight are not generally valid."
    },
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "The reported RAAN value does not indicate the launch facility location; RAAN specifies the orientation of the orbital plane relative to the reference direction. Launch-site access depends on launch azimuth, launch window, and achievable inclination."
    },
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "The report says the orbit is circular and that argument of perigee and true anomaly are undefined, but then lists argument of latitude as 0° without specifying an epoch or initial state. A complete initial orbit state needs a defined epoch and a consistent set of elements."
    },
    {
      "where": "Launch",
      "issue": "The proposed Vega E launch is presented as a likely 2025 option, although its development and schedule were uncertain; the report itself notes that the vehicle had not been built. A rideshare to a 600 km, 97.79° orbit is not demonstrated to be available."
    },
    {
      "where": "Ground Control & Communications",
      "issue": "The access table includes apparent duplicate access entries and inconsistent date/time ordering, so the stated frequency and mean contact duration should be checked against the STK scenario and report interval."
    },
    {
      "where": "Ground Control & Communications",
      "issue": "The claimed high pass frequency from a more polar ground station is not a sufficient basis for selecting a site. Access depends on station latitude, orbit geometry, elevation mask, and the chosen analysis period; no elevation mask or link budget is given."
    },
    {
      "where": "Mission Duration & EOL",
      "issue": "A drag sail can increase atmospheric drag, but the report gives no sail area, mass, ballistic coefficient, deployment reliability, or orbit-decay estimate to show that the spacecraft will deorbit within an acceptable timeframe."
    },
    {
      "where": "Orbit",
      "issue": "The report identifies 600 km altitude and orbital inclination but does not substantiate the imaging design with ground sample distance, sensor performance, swath, or revisit analysis; the stated whole-reef daily coverage is not demonstrated."
    }
  ],
  "overall": "The submission presents a coherent preliminary mission concept and meaningful STK-based orbit and access work. It addresses most required topics, but several design claims are unsupported or technically imprecise, the launch option is speculative, and the EOL and imaging performance are not quantitatively validated. Supporting software files and stronger citation and figure conventions are needed."
}
